  "kind": "statement",
  "title": "Closed points are Cartier divisors on smooth curves",
  "statement": "Let $C$ be a smooth connected curve over $k$ and\n$P\\in C$ a closed point. Then $P$, with its reduced structure,\nis an effective Cartier divisor on $C$: locally at $P$, the ideal\nof $P$ is generated by one non-zero-divisor. Consequently every\neffective divisor of [def-sc-divisor] is an effective Cartier\ndivisor.",
  "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/master/blueprint/sec-picard-framework/sk-thm-picard-duality/lem-sc-point-cartier.md",
    "place": "node lem-sc-point-cartier, order 694"
  },
  "stmt_deps": [
    "def-sc-divisor",
    "def-sc-curves"
  ],
  "proof": [
    {
      "text": "By [lem-sc-smooth-regular], $\\mathcal O_{C,P}$ is a regular\nlocal ring of dimension one, hence a discrete valuation ring by\n[lem-sc-regular-dim1-dvr]; its maximal ideal $\\mathfrak m_P$\nis generated by a uniformizer $t_P$, a non-zero-divisor. The\nideal of $P$ in $\\mathcal O_{C,P}$ is $\\mathfrak m_P=(t_P)$, so\n$P=V(t_P)$ on an affine neighbourhood where $t_P$ extends; hence\n$P$ is an effective Cartier divisor. A finite sum of effective\nCartier divisors is effective Cartier (locally, multiply the defining\nequations), which gives the last assertion.",
      "uses": [
        "lem-sc-smooth-regular",
        "lem-sc-regular-dim1-dvr"
      ],
      "elementary": false
    }
  ],
  "proof_deps": [
    "lem-sc-smooth-regular",
